  "semantic_checklist": [
    {
      "id": "C01",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The attached printed pages 4–6 contain equation (1.10), the discontinuity discussion, normalized cell averages, and the workflow following (1.11).",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation combines rectangle conservation, spatial averages, local Riemann information, numerical fluxes, and a one-step update.",
      "reasoning": "The selected passage and inherited conservation context match the comparison. Source identification uses the supplied hash and rendered pages; no independent hash computation is claimed."
    },
    {
      "id": "C02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source uses vector conserved quantities, a physical flux, neighboring cell states, and real spatial and temporal variables.",
      "translation_evidence": "States and fluxes lie in positive-dimensional real vector spaces; incidence maps supply neighboring states and selected left and right faces.",
      "reasoning": "These types support the selected local construction. Abstract cell and face indices do not require a global mesh theorem for this local calculation."
    },
    {
      "id": "C03",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The workflow obtains information from the neighboring-state Riemann problem. The selected coordinator convention permits conditional error bounds without a book-specified tolerance.",
      "translation_evidence": "Reference witnesses precede the universally quantified old-average and reference-to-physical flux bounds.",
      "reasoning": "The references remain fixed while comparison bounds vary. No witness is improperly selected after seeing a desired error tolerance."
    },
    {
      "id": "C04",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "The printed passage seeks numerical fluxes approximating physical fluxes. The coordinator convention permits exact or approximate information-only routines and independent error bounds.",
      "translation_evidence": "D027 additionally requires every permitted equal-state problem to produce exactly the physical flux.",
      "reasoning": "Exact equal-state consistency is an additional applicability restriction absent from the selected passage and both recorded interpretations. It is not needed for the displayed conditional error estimate."
    },
    {
      "id": "C05",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Cell averages are normalized integrals, and Riemann-derived information supports both numerical flux computation and a time-step update.",
      "translation_evidence": "The conclusions include normalized averages, the solve/extract/flux pipeline, the update, an exact error identity, and a conditional next-step error bound.",
      "reasoning": "The requested local conclusions are represented under the coordinator-selected quantitative interpretation. The inherited discontinuity discussion supplies conservation semantics rather than an additional separately selected theorem."
    },
    {
      "id": "C06",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) uses interval integrals and physical endpoint fluxes; Section 1.2 divides cell integrals by cell volume.",
      "translation_evidence": "D002, D018–D024, D029, and the integration and norm entries explain normalization, oriented Bochner integrals, rectangular balance, and the update.",
      "reasoning": "The supplied translation and dependency ledger give explicit meanings for the relevant operators. This judgment uses those meanings without inspecting Lean."
    },
    {
      "id": "C07",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "The source gives qualitative numerical approximation; the coordinator convention replaces that ambiguity with explicit conditional bounds.",
      "translation_evidence": "Numerical approximation is expressed by norm inequalities, but equal neighboring states additionally force exact equality with the physical flux.",
      "reasoning": "The error inequalities fit the selected interpretation. The unconditional equality on permitted equal-state problems introduces a distinct stronger method requirement."
    },
    {
      "id": "C08",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The physical flux and the numerical flux have distinct roles, and available cell averages can be approximate.",
      "translation_evidence": "U(c), Q_s, Q_t, F, G, and H separately represent old numerical states, exact spatial averages, numerical fluxes, reference fluxes, and comparison-field physical fluxes.",
      "reasoning": "The error decomposition preserves these distinctions and does not silently identify local reference solutions with the comparison field."
    },
    {
      "id": "C09",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) subtracts right endpoint flux from left endpoint flux; (1.11) assigns the left and right neighboring states in that order.",
      "translation_evidence": "The update subtracts Δt/Δx times right-minus-left numerical flux, with the selected cell on the right of its left face and the left of its right face.",
      "reasoning": "Signs, incidence assignments, positive duration, and cell-width normalization agree. The discrete coefficient is an interpretation-level construction rather than a printed formula."
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "status": "fail",
      "source_evidence": "The selected workflow and coordinator convention allow approximate Riemann information routines without specifying exact equal-state behavior.",
      "translation_evidence": "All quantified method objects must satisfy exact equal-state consistency throughout their permitted domains.",
      "reasoning": "The translation specializes the admissible method class. That reduced applicability cannot be classified as a stronger faithful result merely because consistency also appears in the conclusion."
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.11) leaves the value at zero unspecified. The neighboring-cell construction does not require distinct states. The inherited receipt adopts rectangle conservation with explicit integrability.",
      "translation_evidence": "Reference initial data leave the origin unrestricted, allow equal states, require positive averaging intervals, and impose integral rather than classical solutionhood.",
      "reasoning": "The origin and nondegenerate averaging conventions are compatible. The extra numerical requirement in the equal-state case is addressed separately in C04 and C07."
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source includes linear hyperbolic problems and neighboring-cell Riemann solves.",
      "translation_evidence": "Positive dimension, inhabited selected cells and faces, admissible old states, and permitted face problems are required.",
      "reasoning": "Scalar linear advection with constant admissible data and an exact consistent flux supplies a nonvacuous instance. Empty method domains cannot satisfy the selected-face hypotheses."
    },
    {
      "id": "N01",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The paragraph following (1.11) describes solving a neighboring-state problem, obtaining information, computing a numerical flux, and updating averages.",
      "translation_evidence": "The pipeline explicitly composes a dependent solver, information extraction, numerical flux evaluation, and the conservative update.",
      "reasoning": "The execution dependencies are retained, and the solver result need not contain a complete solution field."
    },
    {
      "id": "N02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The book specifies no error norm. The coordinator convention expressly avoids attributing a chosen norm to the book.",
      "translation_evidence": "Every error uses the maximum-coordinate norm on real vectors.",
      "reasoning": "This is a concrete implementation of the selected conditional error interpretation, not evidence that the source prescribed this norm."
    },
    {
      "id": "N03",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The coordinator convention calls for independent old-average and time-averaged physical face-flux errors yielding a next-step bound.",
      "translation_evidence": "The final bound combines the old-average error with numerical-to-reference and reference-to-physical face-flux errors.",
      "reasoning": "The triangle-inequality decomposition supplies the selected one-step error notion without claiming stability, convergence, or an unconditional accuracy guarantee."
    },
    {
      "id": "N04",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Approximate cell averages and numerical fluxes approximate their physical counterparts.",
      "translation_evidence": "The exact error identity retains old-state error and numerical-versus-physical boundary flux discrepancies.",
      "reasoning": "Exact and computed quantities remain distinct throughout the estimate, subject to the separate extra equal-state requirement."
    },
    {
      "id": "N05",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "The selected passage gives no asymptotic expansion, convergence order, or remainder formula.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation states an exact identity and a finite conditional inequality.",
      "reasoning": "There are no asymptotic terms or limiting regimes to compare."
    },
    {
      "id": "N06",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The passage does not provide universal solvability or uniqueness hypotheses. The coordinator convention permits conditional exact or approximate routines; the inherited receipt supplies rectangle-conservation semantics.",
      "translation_evidence": "Reference existence is assumed through the method contract on its domain; uniqueness, entropy selection, and self-similarity are not concluded.",
      "reasoning": "The translation avoids deriving universal well-posedness from the introductory prose. Reference conservation is interpreted using the applicable inherited convention, not attributed to an explicit analytic formulation in the book."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "translation_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "no",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded coordinator-selected interpretation and the applicable inherited rectangle-conservation convention, the translation covers only methods satisfying an additional exact equal-state consistency contract. The selected interpretation does not impose that restriction. For example, for scalar linear advection f(u)=u, an information routine returning the exact local mean flux plus a fixed small nonzero δ admits constant-state conservative references and a numerical-to-reference error bound |δ|. Independent physical face-flux and old-average bounds still yield the selected conditional update estimate. Nevertheless, on equal states k this routine returns k+δ rather than k and cannot inhabit the translated method class. Consequently the translated proposition does not preserve the full applicability of the interpreted workflow."
    },
    "source_implies_translation": {
      "verdict": "yes",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded interpretations, and after imposing the translation's additional method contract, its conditional conclusions follow. The method contract supplies the reference witnesses, reference error bounds, and exact equal-state consistency. The inherited rectangle balance yields the normalized physical update, subtraction gives the displayed error identity, and the triangle inequality gives the final maximum-norm estimate. This direction concerns the translated conditional proposition; it does not mean that the book or either recorded convention asserts exact consistency for every approximate routine."
    }
  },
  "classification": "not-faithful-weaker",
  "accepted": false,
  "requires_adjudication": true,
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "additional-method-hypothesis",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 5 describes numerical flux approximation and the neighboring-state information workflow. Printed page 6 permits approximate Riemann solvers. The coordinator-selected interpretation supplies conditional error bounds but does not require exact equal-state consistency.",
      "translation_evidence": "D027 and the translated hypotheses require N(extract(solve(p)))=f(p.left) whenever a permitted problem has equal left and right states.",
      "impact": "The added contract excludes approximate information routines whose equal-state flux error is small and explicitly bounded. The conditional next-step estimate remains meaningful for such routines, so the restriction reduces applicability. Adjudication should resolve whether an additional restriction to exactly consistent methods is authorized; it is not established by the supplied evidence."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "interpretation-qualified-comparison",
      "source_evidence": "The printed passage specifies neither a discrete update formula nor quantitative accuracy bounds, and leaves the analytic interpretation of conservation at discontinuities implicit.",
      "translation_evidence": "The translation uses rectangle-conservative references, a Δt/Δx update, and a maximum-norm error estimate.",
      "impact": "These portions are assessed under the separately recorded coordinator interpretation and inherited user receipt. Their compatibility must not be reported as literal equivalence to an explicitly printed quantitative theorem."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The translation preserves normalization, flux signs, neighboring-state assignments, information-only solver outputs, and the conditional one-step error calculation under the recorded interpretations. Its material mismatch is the additional exact equal-state consistency requirement on every permitted method problem. Because that premise excludes otherwise eligible approximate routines, the result has reduced applicability rather than genuine nonvacuous additional strength. Adjudication is requested for the authorization and scope of this extra restriction."
}
